# Scorecards builder — prefer `.builder-main`

This rule is for **builder UI** work (Figma or layout in the create/edit scorecard screen). It does **not** apply to autoplay films, cursor scripts, timing, or other non-builder edits in `index.vue` — those follow [autoplay-demo](../skills/autoplay-demo/SKILL.md) and the prototype’s `CLAUDE.md`.

When implementing builder UI, put the design in `.builder-main` inside **the Scorecards copy you are editing** (`src/prototypes/scorecards-*/components/BuilderView.vue`). That is the page body (breadcrumbs, title, actions, question list, detail). Prefer that over rewriting chrome.

## Leave chrome alone unless Ted asks

- Global left nav (`LeftBar`)
- Admin sidebar (`.builder-admin-nav`)
- Call Review and Analytics views

Figma may show an older sidebar, left menu, or shell. **Ignore those differences.** The live chrome in that prototype is the source of truth.

## Old Figma, modern visuals

Figma for `.builder-main` may also be old (wrong button color/size, darker input borders, pre-Dialtone styling). Keep the **layout and intent**. Restyle controls to current Dialtone using the live Scorecards prototype you are editing and [dialtone.dialpad.com](https://dialtone.dialpad.com/).

Do **not** ship a version that looks meaningfully older or off-system. If the file would require that, **ask first**. The user may say the file is old — treat that as “take the spirit, update the visual language.”

## When to ask

- Chrome difference looks **major** (missing a whole nav, a different product shell)
- A `.builder-main` control would look older or unlike Dialtone / the existing prototype

Default: ignore outdated chrome; modernize outdated builder-main styling.
